#: library/processes/translation-review-adjudication.bpmn:20
#. Diagram element documentation
msgid "A person's word on a translation, carried from the page they read it on to the record. Candidate translations come from any engine and from anyone; a reader opens the review page or a block's 🌐 pane, accepts what is there, or picks a ranked candidate or writes their own; that travels as a GitHub issue; a workflow checks it and opens a pull request; a maintainer merges it. You are in this process when a catalogue has a translation nobody has adjudicated: a `machine`, `flagged` or `stale` cell on the review page, or a string a block's 🌐 pane shows as `proposed`, `agent`, `mt`, `missing` or `stale`. `translation-workflow` produces the machine translation and `human-translation-workflow` makes a locale official; this process sits between them and does neither. What it records is that ONE catalogue was read by ONE person who could be held to it, against the exact bytes they saw. Two rules hold throughout. Candidates are never a barrier to having a translation: an empty `msgstr` gets the first candidate, marked fuzzy, so the page always has something to show. And a reader's text is a proposal until a collaborator chooses it: anyone may propose, and every proposal is appended to the candidates sidecar beside the others; only an owner, member or collaborator adjudicates, by choosing one text as the translation with a reason. A string nobody has adjudicated shows the best-ranked candidate, fuzzy and unofficial, unless an official source's English matches the msgid exactly. Locale sign-off (`X-Folio-Official`, the locale's `status.json`) is a separate, deliberate act that this process never performs. GitHub is one Tool here, not the process: the issue is the carrier of the verdict and the pull request the carrier of the record. Another forge carrying the same `translation-adjudication/v1` record would be another Tool satisfying the same skill."
msgstr ""

